    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Nissan Returns to the Annual SEMA Show Featuring a 300-HP Sentra Spec-V Revival Concept, Two Z Cars\n1980 Porsche 911 SC Transformed Into a Track-Focused Performance Car Is Ready For a New Owner\n10 photos\nPhoto: Garage Kept Motors / Instagram\nSitting proudly in the inventory of Grand Rapids, Michigan-based Garage Kept Motors , this air-cooled 1980 Porsche 911 SC is now a feisty track machine ready to dominate its opponents.\nThe Porsche Mission S, the latest concept from the premium German automaker, was shown off cruising the streets of New York City , where it also had its world premiere, and fans are already checking out their bank accounts to see if there’s enough cash for an example. That’s the future of the sports car brand, for sure, but what about the past?\nWell, some folks only have eyes for the air-cooled era, and here’s an example to quench that thirst – a 1980 Porsche 911 SC that has been thoroughly modified. The automaker first introduced the new version of the 911, called the 911 SC, back in 1978, with the moniker appearing for the first time since the 356SC – with the moniker allegedly standing for Super Carrera.\nThis unit retains the unmistakable profile and personality of the classic air-cooled 911 but with a side of performance for the track, and it’s finished in a great-looking shade of baby blue over a matching interior with a single black and carbon fiber Sparco racing bucket seat nestled inside the roll caged interior.\nIt is motivated by a 3.2L short-stroke flat-six, jumping toward a “significant performance upgrade over the original 911 SC engine,” and it’s paired with a Porsche 915-series five-speed manual transmission, allowing for a “particularly engaging combination on a winding road or circuit.” The exterior and interior are all about track use, with an aggressive aerodynamic treatment, prominent No. 54 competition graphics, and a stripped-out cabin\nThere’s also an extensive list of modifications, which includes Corolla Rods, Mod S Cam, and Headers, new windows, belts, and a fuel cell, adjustable Koni sport shocks, a new one-inch anti-roll bar at the front and rear, fiberglass doors, Forgeline wheels, PMO carburetors, and 911 Turbo brake calipers, plus Chris Foley of Tangerine Racing Stage 1 mufflers.\nOf course, “with its 3.2L short-stroke flat-six, 915 5-speed manual transmission, full roll cage, Sparco racing seat, aggressive aerodynamic package, and competition-inspired appearance, this 1980 Porsche 911 SC is a purpose-built interpretation of the classic air-cooled 911. It offers the look and feel of a vintage Porsche transformed into a serious track-day machine,” according to the dealership.\nThat also means it won’t sell cheap – which is why we’re not surprised by the $59,900 asking price. So, what do you think? Is it worth the hustle, or should we look for something else that can be used both on and off the streets and tracks of the world, like the Toyota GR Corolla, for example?\nView this post on Instagram A post shared by Garage Kept Motors LLC (@garagekeptmotors)\nVia // Garage Kept Motors\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n1980 Porsche 911 SC Porsche 911 Porsche 911 SC sports car pricing for sale Garage Kept Motors\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Porsche and La Marzocco Shake Hands Again for Home Brewing Station – Homage to the Carrera RS 2.7",
